 # Native CLI journey specification
 
+NAV-006 acceptance 3's native table subset uses `touchTablesJourney` in `tests/qualification/ui-convergence/tables-journey.mjs`, called by the existing `real core-wiki and touch-member journeys at 390px` registration. An ordinary member creates a populated four-column table through native HTTP, edits an offscreen heading and cell, adds and removes a selected row and column, merges a populated rectangle, edits its anchor, unmerges and edits a previously covered cell. A second populated merge at the far-right edge repeats anchor editing and unmerge after horizontal scrolling, including a covered coloured cell that retains its visible colour and accepts another touch edit after unmerge. Every operation reconciles complete native stored content before and after page reload, preserving untouched cells, row identities, widths, colour and caption. Reachability uses real touch taps with visual-viewport hit testing, and each edited input must fit wholly inside the table's horizontal scroll viewport even when its cell spans several columns. The merged and unmerged screens check overflow and serious or critical accessibility findings. Existing desktop spreadsheet paste, keyboard structure, sorting and database round-trip assertions remain unchanged. This supporting journey does not establish complete gesture substitutions, every operator or block surface, native apps or R-9 human review; NAV-006 remains partial.
+
 `tests/local-journey/cli.test.mjs` runs the exact case “fresh README CLI setup, native sign-in at both widths, custody, transfer and reset” through a fresh source clone and the public rer commands. Its supporting scope includes WORK-013’s native `/setup` page, unfinished app-route redirect and completed redirect at 1440 and 390, with actual wizard completion. It also exercises setup idempotency and private-byte custody, key-gated native `RER_OPEN` sign-in, dev and preview lifecycle, confirmed reset and a D1/R2 installation archive round trip.
 
 `tests/release/delivery-runtime.mjs` separately checks the unpacked delivery Worker through HTTP: an unfinished authenticated owner follows `/app` to `/setup`, where the document renders the current identity; real native first-run completion makes `/app` render the workspace and `/setup` return to `/app`. Native page editing, search, readable export, profile boundaries and served asset bytes remain checked. Its identity fixture and test-only mail transport substitution establish local packaged-runtime evidence, not empty-store bootstrap, browser, provider or hosted acceptance.
